# `this.cells[square + step]` in a loop over written steps. `square` is declared a number, so the key is a number of which nothing says that it is whole. An element read under such a key had no typed form: its level was
# unknown, it might run other code, and in the typed copy of the loop the walk began the turn again in the copy as written AT THE READ, ON THE USUAL WAY: the first square on the board ended the typed copy for the rest of the loop.
# Now, in a turn of a typed copy that may still begin again, the key of an element of a list DECLARED TO HOLD NUMBERS ALONE is asked for an int32; where it is none (1.5, NaN, 2 ** 32) the turn begins again as written; behind
# the test the read is typed as under any whole key (made).
# A key that goes round the loop, the head's own parameter or a step of it, is left to the counters, which hold it to an int32 by themselves (round). A list of texts has no such form: nothing is asked (texts).
